## 1. The inversion

| | variant-shard (`shard_by`, shipped) | **sample-shard (`shard_samples`, this)** |
|---|---|---|
| rows of the input | variants | variants (unchanged) |
| split axis | variants (rows) | **samples (genotype columns)** |
| shared companion | one root `.psam` | **one root `.pvar`** |
| per-shard files | `.pgen` + `.pvar` | **`.pgen` + `.psam`** |
| `concat_dim` | `"variants"` | `"samples"` |
| how rows map to shards | a row's key column value | **every row goes to every shard**, sliced to that shard's sample columns |

The key engineering difference falls out of the last row: a variant-shard holds
**one** open writer and rolls to the next on a key change; a sample-shard holds
**N** writers open at once and feeds **every** variant row to all N, each taking
its own sample slice (§6).

## 2. Input contract

- **Variant-orient input only.** Rows are variants; the genotypes column holds
  all N samples. Reject sample-orient input with an error — this writer does
  **no transpose** (`.pgen` is variant-major; a transpose is out of scope).
- **Sample identity = the ordered IID list**, resolved exactly as the plain
  bundle does (`ResolveIdentity`, `pfile_writer.cpp:1141`): STRUCT genotypes →
  field names are the IIDs; else a `samples ['id1', …]` option; a bare
  `TINYINT[N]` LIST is rejected (no identity). The split partitions **this
  list**.
- **The slice** must handle each genotype shape the bundle accepts: a STRUCT
  column (slice = a contiguous subset of struct fields), and a LIST/`samples`
  column (slice = a contiguous index range of the per-variant genotype vector).
  Check `TryStructIidsFromSchema` / `ResolveIdentity` for the accepted set.
- `shard_samples` present ⇒ sample-shard mode. `shard_by` present ⇒ variant
  mode (shipped). **Both present ⇒ reject**, with an error naming the V2 grid
  follow-on (§8). Neither ⇒ the plain single bundle.

## 3. The catalog output — the merge contract

Emit **exactly** the shape P13-008's reader is tested against
(`test/data/pfile_catalog_le_samples.pfile.json` on `feature/P13-008-sample-concat`),
not only the prose in `docs/formats/pfile_catalog.md`:

```json
{
  "pfile_catalog": 1,
  "dataset": "<name>",
  "concat_dim": "samples",
  "shared": { "pvar": "<shared>.pvar" },
  "files": [
    { "prefix": "<shard>", "psam": "<shard>.psam",
      "partition": { "start": <lo>, "stop": <hi>, "source_ordinal": <i> } }
  ]
}
```

- `partition.start`/`stop` are **global sample-axis ordinals** (half-open:
  shard i covers `[start, stop)`; the first shard starts at 0; `stop` of the
  last = total sample count). Contiguous and gap-free, in file order.
- `shared.pvar` is the one `.pvar` written once.
- Each `files[i].psam` is that shard's own `.psam` (its IID slice).
- `.pgen` is implied by `prefix` (the per-shard `.pgen`), as in the v1 variant
  catalog.

**Open sub-question (settle against the fixture at build, not now):** whether
`files[i]` also carries an explicit `pvar` omission vs. relying on `shared`.
The fixture omits per-file `pvar`; match it. Verify field-for-field with
`git show feature/P13-008-sample-concat:test/data/pfile_catalog_le_samples.pfile.json`
as the first build step.

## 4. THE SPLIT SPEC — DECIDED: C, named IID groups (Teague, 2026-11-08)

> **Decision.** `shard_samples {label: ['iid', …], …}` — a map of shard label →
> IID list. The key is the shard's directory/prefix label (mirroring the variant
> sharder's `<col>=<value>` hive dirs). The IID lists must partition the input
> sample set **exactly** (every IID once, none missing/extra) and each must be a
> **contiguous run** of the input sample order; a gap, overlap, reorder, or
> missing/extra IID is a clean BinderException. The recommendation below stands
> as the rationale.

There is **no sample attribute** to shard *by*: the `.psam` is IID-only today
(`sample_fields {sex,…}` is an explicit "not yet supported" error). So the split
is an **explicit, contiguous partition of the ordered IID list**. Three
candidates, separated by three constraints — a per-shard **directory/prefix
label**, **self-checking** against the IIDs, and **contiguity** (the catalog's
partition ranges are global ordinals; a non-contiguous group would reorder the
output samples and break the round-trip):

| option | form | label | self-check | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A. sizes** | `shard_samples [1, 2, 5]` | invented (`shard0…`) | none (a size list can't catch an off-by-one vs. the IIDs) | weakest |
| **B. IID groups** | `shard_samples [['s1'], ['s2','s3'], …]` | invented | **exact** (must partition the IID set) | ok, no labels |
| **C. named IID groups** (recommend) | `shard_samples {a: ['s1'], b: ['s2','s3'], c: […]}` | **the key** (`<label>=`-style, like the hive variant dirs) | **exact** | best |

**Recommendation: C** — named IID groups. It gives real directory/prefix labels
(mirroring the variant sharder's `<col>=<value>` hive dirs), partitions the IID
set exactly (self-checking against `ResolveIdentity`'s list), and the writer
enforces contiguity (each group's IIDs must be a contiguous run in the input
order; else a clear error). **Default if unspecified:** none — `shard_samples`
has no safe default split, so it is required when sample-shard mode is entered
(exactly as `shard_by` names its column).

## 5. Non-goals

- No transpose; no sample-orient input.
- No `sample_fields` metadata in the per-shard `.psam` (still IID-only; the
  "not yet supported" error stands). Attribute-driven sharding waits on that.
- No V2 grid (both axes at once) — §8, the next pencil-in.
- `union`/`intersect` are a reader concern, not here.

## 6. The engineering — N writers open at once

`PfileWriteGlobalState` today stages **one** fileset (one `PvarWriteGlobalState`
+ one `PgenWriteGlobalState` + one `.psam`), promotes with `.pgen` last, and
cleans that one on failure. Sample-shard mode needs **N `.pgen` writers + N
staging `.psam` + one shared `.pvar`**, all open across the whole row stream.
Specify:

- **State.** A vector of N per-shard sink states (each a staging `.pgen` +
  staging `.psam` + the sample-column index range), plus one staging `.pvar`
  (written from the row stream once, not per shard) and the catalog path.
- **Sink.** For each input chunk, write the variant row to the shared `.pvar`
  once, then to each shard's `.pgen` the genotype sub-vector for that shard's
  sample range. (The `.psam` files are out-of-band from the IID slices, written
  at init like the plain bundle.)
- **Promote order.** All staging promoted only on full success:
  `.pvar` → every shard's `.psam` → every shard's `.pgen` (**each `.pgen`
  last within its shard**, matching the shipped single-shard ordering) → the
  `.pfile.json` catalog **last of all** (so a half-written cohort has no
  catalog → ATTACH/`read_pfile_catalog` sees "not there", never half-new).
- **Cleanup.** The destructor and the finalize-failure path remove **all N**
  shards' staging (`.pgen`/`.pgen.tmp`/`.psam`) + the shared `.pvar` staging +
  the catalog staging. Extend the existing `CleanupPartial` to loop N.
- **Memory.** N open `STPgenWriter`s is N× the single-writer footprint. Document
  it; do not cap in v1 (the variant sharder does not cap shard count either),
  but note a cap as a follow-on if N is large.

